{
  "title": "Issue queue: canonicalize snapshot keys and invalidate only on decision-relevant run changes (follow-up to #976)",
  "body": "## Problem
Review of #976 (the batched issue-queue lookup) found two defects in its new guard, which defers an issue whose linked run changed during the poll. Both were reproduced. #976 landed first, at the owner's decision, because it ends 7–34 s daemon stalls and neither defect double-starts work.

1. **Case-variant repository keys.** Snapshots are keyed by the configured repository string, case-sensitively (src/integrations/issue-queue.ts ~304), while invalidations use lowercase keys and are cleared by the next snapshot. With `owner/repo` and `OWNER/REPO` both configured, a competing run that finishes between the two reads is missed, and a stale snapshot admits another run. Fix: canonicalize repository keys everywhere in the queue, and deduplicate configured repositories case-insensitively at config load.
2. **Accounting-only publications defer work indefinitely.** The listener (~237) invalidates an issue on every publication of a linked run, without comparing the fields that matter, and admission (~391) then refuses with "priority re-approval required". `refreshRunTotals` on an already completed run changes neither linkage, status nor finish time, yet defers admission, and repeating it each poll defers it forever. Fix: invalidate only when status, `finishedAt` or linkage changes. Also change the refusal text to a temporary one, such as "linked run changed during the poll; retrying next poll", because no re-approval is needed.

## Tests
Add review's two regressions; each fails at #976's head:

```ts
test("case-variant repository snapshots cannot clear another candidate's invalidation", async () => {
  const h = harness({}, ["owner/repo", "OWNER/REPO"]);
  const repo = h.store.getRepoBySlug("owner/repo");
  if (!repo) throw new Error("missing repo");
  h.data.set("owner/repo", [issue()]);
  h.data.set("OWNER/REPO", [issue()]);
  let competingId = "";
  h.intercept = (args) => {
    if (!args[3]?.includes('name:"REPO"') || competingId) return;
    const run = h.store.createRun(repo, {
      repo: repo.slug,
      prompt: "Competing start",
      source: "github",
      sourceRef: { kind: "issue", repo: repo.slug, number: 1 },
    });
    competingId = run.id;
    h.store.updateRun(run.id, { status: "cancelled", finishedAt: Date.parse(at) + 1000 });
  };
  h.start();
  await h.advance();
  expect(competingId).not.toBe("");
  expect(h.runs().map((run) => run.id)).toEqual([competingId]);
});

test("metadata publications do not starve an approved issue across polls", async () => {
  const h = harness({}, ["owner/a", "owner/b"]);
  const repo = h.store.getRepoBySlug("owner/a");
  if (!repo) throw new Error("missing repo");
  const previous = h.store.createRun(repo, { repo: repo.slug, prompt: "Closes #1" });
  h.store.updateRun(previous.id, { status: "cancelled", finishedAt: Date.parse(at) - 1000 });
  h.data.set(repo.slug, [issue()]);
  let publications = 0;
  h.intercept = (args) => {
    if (!args[3]?.includes('name:"b"')) return;
    h.store.refreshRunTotals(previous.id);
    publications++;
  };
  h.start();
  await h.advance();
  await h.advance(180_000);
  expect(publications).toBe(2);
  expect(h.runs().filter((run) => run.sourceRef?.via === "queue")).toHaveLength(1);
});
